braam — the commands
====================

The shell looks for a command word in three places: shell functions, then the
builtins below, then files on PATH — /bin:/pkg/bin at boot. Only a file costs a
process: a wasm binary in a worker of its own, with its own memory limit, which
^C kills.

Builtins live inside /bin/sh. echo, test, true and false are files in /bin as
well, so anything can spawn them; [ and : are builtins only.

This list is the file /etc/help, and covers what the system ships with. Every
program also prints how it is spelled and exits 2 when you get it wrong.

Shell builtins
--------------

  .         <file> — run a file's commands in this shell
  :         [<arg>...] — do nothing, and succeed
  [         <expr> ] — test a condition, the same as `test`
  break     [<n>] — leave the loop, or n of them
  cd        [<dir>] — change directory, to /home by default
  command   -v <name>... — show what a name would run
  continue  [<n>] — go on to the loop's next turn
  echo      [-n] [<word>...] — print the arguments
  eval      [<arg>...] — join the arguments and run them as a command
  exec      [<command>] — run a command in place of the shell, or just keep its
            redirections
  exit      [<status>] — end the shell
  export    [<name>[=<value>]...] — pass a variable to child processes
  false     do nothing, and fail
  fg        [%n] — bring a background job to the front and wait for it
  jobs      list the jobs started with &
  kill      %n — stop a job
  read      <name>... — read one line and split it across the names
  readonly  [<name>[=<value>]...] — stop a variable being changed
  return    [<status>] — leave a function or a sourced file
  set       [-eux] [--] [<arg>...] — set options or parameters, or list
            variables
  shift     [<n>] — drop the first n positional parameters
  test      <expr> — test a condition; the result is the exit status
  trap      [<action>|-] 0|2 — run an action when the shell ends (0) or on ^C
            (2)
  true      do nothing, and succeed
  unset     [-f] <name>... — remove a variable, or a function with -f
  wait      [%n...] — wait for one background job, or for all of them

Programs in /bin
----------------

  cat       [<file>...] — print files, or the input
  chat      <url> [<nick>] — chat with whoever else is on a WebSocket
  clear     clear the screen
  curl      [-i] [-X <method>] [-H <header>] [-d <data>] <url> — fetch a URL
  date      [-u] — print the date and time
  df        [-h] — show filesystem space, scaled with -h
  echo      [-n] [<word>...] — print the arguments
  edit      <file> — full-screen editor; ^S saves, ^Q quits
  env       [-i] [<name>=<value>...] [<command> [<arg>...]]
            — print the environment, or run a command with it changed
  false     do nothing, and fail
  fexport   <file> [<name>] — download a file to your computer
  fimport   [<dir>] — copy files from your computer into /import
  grep      [-i] [-v] <text> [<file>...] — print the lines that match
  head      [-n <count>] [<file>...] — the first lines, ten by default
  help      show this list, which is /etc/help
  hog       take memory until the limit stops it
  less      [<file>] — read a file a screen at a time; q quits
  ln        -s [-f] <target> <name> — make a symbolic link
  ls        [-1CRSdhlrt] [<path>...] — list files, in columns on a terminal
  mkdir     [-p] <dir>... — make directories, with missing parents under -p
  mount     list the mounted filesystems
  mv        [-fi] <src>... <dst> — move or rename files
  pbcopy    [<file>...] — copy the input to the clipboard
  pbpaste   print the clipboard
  pkg       [-v] <command> [<arg>...] — install and remove packages
  ps        list the tasks the kernel is running, and which are processes
  pwd       print the current directory
  rm        [-r] <path>... — remove files, or directories with -r
  sh        [-eux] [-s | -c <command> | <file>] [<arg>...] — the shell
  sleep     [-m] <secs> — wait, in seconds, or milliseconds with -m
  spin      [<millions>] — loop without stopping, so you can kill it
  tail      [-n <count>] [<file>...] — the last lines, ten by default
  test      <expr> — test a condition; the result is the exit status
  timeout   [-m] <secs> <command> [<arg>...]
            — run a command, and kill it after a delay (124)
  touch     <file>... — make empty files, or update an existing one's mtime
  true      do nothing, and succeed
  uname     [-a|-s|-r|-m] — name the system, and with -a the host it runs on
  unzip     [-l] [-p] [-d <dir>] <archive> [<name>...]
            — extract a zip, list it with -l, or print one entry with -p
  vmstat    [-s] [-m] [<secs> [<count>]] — what the kernel is doing, as rates
  watch     [-m] [-n <secs>] <command> [<arg>...] — run a command again and
            again
  wc        [<file>...] — count lines, words and bytes

Packages
--------

  `pkg` installs into /pkg. A system update does not touch /pkg, so packages
  survive one. /pkg/bin comes after /bin on PATH, so a package cannot replace a
  system command. /etc/repositories holds the repository URL.

  Packages from a repository are always checked. The index is signed with the
  keys in /etc/anchor, and each package must match the size and digest the
  index gives before anything is unpacked. If a check fails, pkg says which one
  and installs nothing.

  You can also install an archive directly — a path ending .zip, or a URL.
  Nothing vouches for it, so pkg warns you, records it as `G: 0`, and `pkg
  verify` calls it `unvouched`. The commands are spelled `pkg <name>`:

  update    download the repository index, and check it
  search    <pattern> — find packages by name or description
  info      <package> — what the index says about one package
  install   <package>... — install packages and what they need. A .zip path or
            a URL is installed without checking
  remove    <package>... — remove packages
  autoremove
            remove packages installed only as dependencies
  upgrade   upgrade everything installed
  list      [-i] — list what the index offers; -i lists what is installed
  files     <package> — the files a package installed
  verify    [<package>] — check installed files against the digests saved at
            install time
  clean     delete downloaded archives and old generations; the one before the
            current is kept
  help      print a short version of this list

  `-v` shows the network traffic — the request, the status, the headers and the
  body's size. It works anywhere on the line.

  An install builds a new generation under /pkg/gen, then moves the symbolic
  link /pkg/active onto it. That is the moment it takes effect. To go back, move
  the link yourself: `ln -sf /pkg/gen/<n> /pkg/active`. There is no
  `pkg rollback`.

  `pkg verify` tells you a file has changed since it was installed. It cannot
  promise that none did: files are checked once, at install, and anything here
  can overwrite anything.

More
----

  /README           what this system is, in a page
  /etc/motd         what boot prints
  /etc/anchor       the keys a repository's index is checked against
  /proc             the system as text — try cat /proc/host

  help | grep ls    off a terminal `less` copies, so this list pipes
